From: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: SDR color management through the DPU output LUTs

Without per-plane color management gamescope only applies its color
pipeline in the composite shader, so a directly scanned-out layer loses
night mode and the color sliders. The msm DPU's post-blend CRTC
DEGAMMA_LUT (256) and 17^3 DPU_3D_LUT are the same shape as the shader's
shaper and 3D LUT, so an SDR gamma 2.2 output can carry that chain in
hardware. Enabling them changes which display blocks the CRTC owns, so
that transition needs a modeset; being post-blend, a translucent overlay
is corrected as the blended pixel. Per-plane offload is the follow-up.

The author's patch unchanged except for the reconciliation with 0014,
which is ours. 0014 answers the same question with the CRTC's CTM alone,
because the DPU exposes no degamma and only a per-channel diagonal is
expressible without one. Both can now be present at once -- only a kernel
with the LUTDMA engine offers DPU_3D_LUT, and the SM8250 boards have no
such engine -- so the two paths are ordered rather than merged:

  - the output LUTs carry the whole chain when they are applied, so the
    CTM is held at the IDENTITY for those frames. drm_update_crtc_ctm()
    is given false, exactly as it is for a composited frame, and for the
    same reason: the transform is already being applied somewhere else,
    and a non-identity matrix would apply it a second time.
  - drm_scanout_is_color_managed() returns true outright, skipping the
    expressibility test. That is the point of the LUTs -- a colour
    temperature change, a gamut mapping and a look LUT are all non-diagonal
    and each one currently costs a full composite for every frame it is
    live.
  - drm_scanout_ctm_is_active() returns false, so partial composition
    stays available. The CTM guard exists because a matrix after blending
    would hit a composited half that already carries the transform; the
    output LUTs have no such split, because the shader leaves the gamma 2.2
    chain unbound on the composite path too and the same post-blend LUTs
    correct both halves.

0014 is therefore the fallback for hardware with no LUT bus, not dead code.

ONE HUNK OF THE AUTHOR'S PATCH IS DELIBERATELY NOT CARRIED, for the same reason.
It added, immediately above our line:

    if ( !SupportsColorManagement() && !outputLuts && shaperLut[EOTF_Gamma22] )
        for each layer: bNeedsFullComposite |= !ColorspaceIsHDR( layer.colorspace );

i.e. every SDR frame composites whenever the output LUTs are not carrying the
chain. That is right in a tree without 0014 -- before it, a scanned-out frame
silently dropped the transform -- and it is REDUNDANT here, because
drm_scanout_is_color_managed() one line below asks the same question and answers
it better: it knows the transform may be a diagonal the CTM can carry. Their
gate is strictly the more conservative of the two, so keeping both would let it
win everywhere and make 0014 unreachable -- including on the SM8250 boards,
which have no DPU_3D_LUT at all and would therefore composite every SDR frame
forever. Dropping it loses nothing. With the LUTs on both allow scanout; with a
transform that is not expressible both composite; only the diagonal case differs,
and there ours is correct.

MEASURED, Pocket FIT, before this hunk was dropped: with drm_output_luts 0 and
night mode at maximum, one live content plane and an identity CTM -- i.e. the
composite, not the diagonal. That is what this removal fixes.

HW PASS, Pocket FIT (SM8650, engine v3), 2026-09-13. Non-neutral colour
temperature -- a Bradford white-point adaptation, which is non-diagonal and so
cost a full composite on every frame it was live -- now SCANS OUT: two live
content planes with the adaptation in the DPU_3D_LUT and the CTM at identity,
against one plane with the LUTs disabled. Night mode at maximum reprograms the
3D LUT while the CTM stays identity; with the LUTs off it falls back to
diag(1.0, 0.41667, 0) on scanout, which is the hunk removal above doing its job.
Sixteen enable/disable transitions, each a modeset, with zero LUTDMA, CTL_FLUSH,
underrun or SMMU-fault lines.

THE TWO PATHS ARE NOT PIXEL-IDENTICAL, BY CONSTRUCTION, and an operator can see
it. calcScanoutLinearScale() deliberately ignores the SDR gamut mapping, because
direct scanout never applied it; the 3D LUT runs the full calcColorTransform()
chain and does. At the default sdrGamutWideness of 0.5 that mapping is live, so
toggling drm_output_luts under a fixed night mode gives "subtle differences in
some colours, nothing completely off" -- saturated content moves, neutrals do
not. Forcing sdrGamutWideness to 0 makes the mapping the identity and the two
become indistinguishable to the eye, which is the measurement that identifies it.
This is a FIX, not a regression: the composite path always applied the gamut
mapping, so the LUTs make scanout and composite agree, where 0014 left them
differing by exactly this and let the difference appear and disappear as frames
switched paths.
